President Trump says he is well prepared  for that meeting with North Korean  leader Kim jong-eun but he also says  attitude is more important than  preparation Secretary of State Mike  Pompeo says the stakes are high when mr.  Trump and Kim talk about defusing  nuclear tensions we want to achieve a  fundamentally different strategic  relationship between our two countries  we are we believe it's important to take  down the threat from the world a  completely denuclearize North Korea in  exchange for that we're prepared to do  things that provide them two security  assurances that they need Ben Tracy is  in Singapore with more on the  much-anticipated meeting Ben good  morning  a good morning this is no doubt the  Superbowl of political summits and  everyone here is now just waiting for  President Trump and Kim Jong moon to  arrive now over at the capela hotel  where the summit between the two leaders  will take place that's already on  lockdown and closed to the public there  is also a visible security presence at  the hotels where President Trump and Kim  Jong moon will be staying Singapore is  designating the zones around those  hotels and the summit location as  special event areas with stricter  security including special forces  there is also airspace restrictions  including a ban on drones now what is  still unknown is what if anything this  summit will actually accomplish will  this just be a meet and greet between  these two unpredictable leaders or will  they reach an actual agreement on  getting rid of North Korea's nuclear  weapons program and perhaps an official  end to the Korean War now typically the  result of a summit like this is agreed  to before it even begins but that does  not appear to be the case here so that  certainly raises the stakes and  president Trump will get more of that  made-for-tv drama that he seems to like  Vlad Ben Tracy in Singapore thank you  for more on what may come from the  summit we turn to Michael Mazar he's a  senior political scientist at the RAND  Corporation welcome to the table nice to  be here  so to Ben's report what are you  expecting from this meeting a meet and  greet or something substantial so  something in between I think the meet  and greet is the most important part  just to see how they get along it's too  short of a summit and there's not been  enough preparatory work done to it  back to some kind of grand bargain where  everything is resolved but there should  be at least a road map that they can  agree to with maybe some initial steps  in the right direction you talk about a  road map if you look back over time  maybe go back to 1994 or even 2005 we've  had negotiations before with North Korea  what did we learn from that that makes a  certain and what needs to be done now  well a couple of things one is that  North Korea has an incentive to draw  these things out and and delay and find  ways to kind of stretch the negotiations  without actually giving up what it's  trying to achieve but the biggest  question right now is how different Kim  jong-un actually is there are  tantalizing signals that he really cares  more about the economic prospects of his  country than his father or grandfather  what he's willing to give up to get that  right is the situation is that well not  at once not at once the question is and  the president Trump's referred to a  process here so the question is can you  get some initial concessions that give  everybody a sense that North Korea's  moving in the right direction and a  clear plan for over a number of years I  think everybody who studies this  realizes it will take a while but at  least if we have some clear milestones  that we're moving toward we'll know  we're moving in the right direction and  that ought to be enough out of an  initial summit Michael when the  president says that he doesn't need much  preparation for a meeting like this or  that he's been preparing for his entire  life does that give him the upper hand  does that put him at a disadvantage or  perhaps neutral I don't know that really  makes a lot of difference I think  clearly secretary Pompeo has been  working with the North Koreans very  closely it sounds like they have some at  least initial statements agreed and the  North Koreans have given some assurance  of a handful of things they're willing  to agree to but one that we know from  summits is by far the most important  thing is the personal chemistry between  the leaders that comes up whether they  develop trust whether they actually like  each other I don't know if that's  possible in this case but that's the  most important thing I'm going to be  looking for is whether the two leaders  seem to be getting along and  establishing a basis of trust that can  move forward we've heard some talk in  the last week about a rift between  Pompeo and Bolton how does that factor  in when those are two of the closest  advisors to the president right now on  right yeah I mean I don't know to what  extent that's true I think the way it  factors in is it just gives the  president a choice always in those cases  it comes down to the president's  decision and the president will side  with one of the other side of his or  potentially her administration and then  that'll that'll be it unless one of the  other of those people then tries to work  under the table to subvert negotiations  or what the other side is doing I don't  get the sense that president Trump would  tolerate that from anybody so I think  he's made his choice which is to try to  move forward you know we have an open  and democratic society here so we'll  know how successful or unsuccessful this  meeting is how do you think it will play  in North Korea not just with the  hardliners in Kim's regime but the  people will they ever even get a sense  of what is happening in Singapore so  it'll play in two ways one is the way  the regime there wants it to play which  is of course the dominant way they will  broadcast whatever message they want to  and the primary message will be the  United States we forced the United  States to come to the table and they  recognized us in ways they never have  before that'll be their primary message  but there is a lot of information  getting into North Korea these days and  there is a sense that the North Korean  people have economic demands that they  haven't had in the past so there'll be a  short term message but then the  longer-term development of this whether  the North Korean people begin to see  benefits out of it  relief of sanctions that will be the  longer-term more important way in which  a message gets through to the North  Korean people all right all eyes on  Singapore Tuesday Michel Mazar thank you  very much as always CBS News will bring  you coverage of the historic meeting in  Singapore starting with our Sunday  broadcast we will also have updates  throughout the summit Jeff Glor will  anchor the CBS Evening News from  Singapore on Monday and Tuesday
 

